{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 17, :comments ["<b>You shall not show partiality in judgment.</b> A warning to the judge that he not be harsh toward this one and soft toward that one." "<b>As the small so the great you shall hear.</b> That a case involving a perutah should be as beloved to you as a case involving a hundred maneh." "<b>You shall not be afraid of any man.</b> And even if he is mute. <b>For the judgment is Elohim's.</b> What you judge for human beings — the judgment does not belong to man, for if you wrongfully obligate a person, the Holy One, blessed be He, will repay him from another direction, so that you are found to be sinning, since this judgment does not belong to the one you obligated but rather to Elohim, who will repay him for what you caused him to lose. And so Jehoshaphat said (II Chronicles 19:6): ״כי לא לאדם תשפטו כי לה׳ ועמכם בדבר משפט״." "<b>And by way of</b> the Kabbalah: ״for the judgment is Elohim's״ — surely, for He is the Judge of the world, as stated (Psalms 75:8): ״כי אלהים שופט זה ישפיל וזה ירים״ — He receives from the attribute of Gevurah, which is the line of Judgment; and so the term משפט is the judgment that is cut. And therefore the Judge of the world is called, in mercy, by the name Elohim, from the attribute of supernal Gevurah. And because ״Elohim stands in the congregation of El,״ even human beings are called by the shared name Elohim, as stated (Exodus 22:8): ״עד האלהים יבא דבר שניהם״ — no need to mention the angels, who are also called by the shared name Elohim, for they are the judges who govern and judge each nation that is entrusted to them, for all of them receive from the attribute of Gevurah." "<b>And you should</b> understand that because Gevurah is from the left, in the manner of what is written (Ezekiel 1:10): ״ופני שור מהשמאל,״ therefore the Great Sanhedrin is called by the language of (Song of Songs 7:2) ״שררך״; and likewise their house of meeting, where they would cut the judgment, is called לשכת הגזית (the Chamber of Hewn Stone), which is the language of cutting. And this is clear. And what is stated, <b>you shall bring near to me and I will hear it,</b> our Rabbis of blessed memory expounded that this utterance was reckoned for Moses as a punishment, and because of it the ruling concerning the daughters of Zelophehad was hidden from him, as I mentioned there." "<b>And one should</b> wonder: how did Moses sin against the Holy One, blessed be He, with this statement, and what did He see in it to punish him for it? Or what presumption is there in the expression תקריבון אלי — 'you shall bring near to me' — when it was impossible for him to speak otherwise?" "<b>But</b> it appears proper to say that since Moses had already mentioned that judgment belongs to Elohim, and then went back and said תקריבון אלי — as though Moses did not weigh his words carefully and nearly equated himself with his Maker — because of this the Rabbis of blessed memory accounted it to him as a punishment."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 1, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:44:47.397395476Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}